 show you one of the things that we  were doing before  probabilistic genotyping is  um we were doing interpretation of dna  mixtures based on what we call the  major contributor minor contributor  scenario  where the height of the dna peak in an  electrophorogram  is correlated to the amount of dna  present so  in a lot of these cases where you would  expect the victim's dna to be present  under their own fingernails  or if it's a rape case where it's a  vaginal swab you  would expect a lot of the victim's dna  to be present  and therefore the peaks would be higher  and maybe less  dna from the perpetrator would be  present and that would be your minor  contributor and so  if we were looking at a dna mixture we  thought visually this is a clear major  minor contributor scenario we were again  trying to manually interpret that  mixture  and in this case um if you look at this  slide i've got  marker one so this is showing you an  example of an electro paragraph for four  different dna markers  tested if you look at the two peaks  under  marker number one many analysts  would interpret this  as being one person in this dna mixture  has  two copies of allele a  and in under marker three because of the  height of the peak this person has two  copies of allele b  because recall from earlier in this  presentation  that when you have two of the same  alleles that peak sits on top of each  other and it increases the height of the  peak  because there's more copies of that a  little present  um so many analysts would you know  interpret  that part of the mixture on under marker  number four  many analysts would say well the height  of the peaks for allele a  and allele d are higher so that's  clearly the major contributor  and the peak for the allele b and c  under marker four are the minor  contributors so that's how they would  pair the alleles together and separate  the mixture into dna profiles for two  individuals  but you know this can get again when you  when you have multiple different people  looking at  the same dna mixture this can  you can arrive at different conclusions  there's some subjectivity so for the  second marker  many analysts would say okay one  individual in this mixture for this  marker  has an allele a and a we'll see  for marker number three same thing you  know some analysts may say  the major contributor is bb and the  minor contributors  ac for marker four again  a lot of analysts would say there's a  clear major minor contributor there  but then you go back over to the first  marker on the far left hand side of the  screen  and even though most trained dna  analysts would have agreed that the  major contributor in this scenario  has two copies of allele a  when they were deducing the allele  pairings for the minor contributors  some analysts may say they have two  copies of the b  allele and other analysts may say  well i think they have one copy of the a  allele and one  b and then if you look underneath the  second marker  there's several different scenarios  there that different analysts could  arrive at as far as what the allele  pairings are for the minor contributor  so and of course keep in mind that in in  standard case work we're analyzing now  20 different dna markers and you're  going to go through this process for  each marker in the dna profile  and so it's a lot more than just the  four markers that i've shown you on the  screen  in other scenarios so on the previous  slide i was  explaining to you kind of what we  considered in the field a  clear major contributor clear minor  contributor scenario or relatively  straightforward  but the reality is that's not what a lot  of mixtures were looking like  in criminal cases you might in other  words do something  like this where the peak heights are  pretty similar between the peaks for  each marker tested  and so there's no discernible major  minor contributor  so at that point you know and i'll go  back to that slide it gets even  a little more complex in how you're  subjectively  determining which two alleles pair with  which person  for each marker tested so what the  nrc report and the pcash report  basically said  is that we need a better way to do this  we need a better way  to interpret complex dna mixtures  and there's too much subjectivity  involved  with people no matter how well-trained  you are how intelligent you are  so let's let computers do it for it  and so there were software programs  developed to help  interpret complex dna mixtures and need  software programs  do what's called probabilistic  genotyping  where in and i'll go back to this  previous slide  the software programs are looking at  peak heights and various  key components of the electrophilogram  and calculating the probability  of each allele pairing up with another  allele to deduce  the proper what the highest probability  is that  of which allele should be paired  together in this mixture so that you can  deconvolute  the mixture of dna profiles into  separate  individual profiles and two of the  the big probabilistic genotyping  software programs that are currently  being used in casework labs  one is called star mix and the other one  is called true allele  and they both they basically both do the  same thing  um they both are interpreting dna  mixtures based on probabilistic  genotyping  and again it's the less subjective we  can never  completely reduce the subjectivity in  dna mixture interpretation because again  it's a blind process  you know we often say that forensic  science is a historical science because  we are  as analysts we're actually analyzing the  remnants of a crime  we're not actually present when the  crime's being committed when that dna  mixture is being formed so we have to  analyze the data after the fact and  that's what these software programs do  and again the less objective less  subjective approach is what we were  shooting for because we're also dealing  with some  unconscious bias sometimes with human  analysts  but the previous way we were doing it  again involved  calculation of what's called a cpi or  cpe  combined probability of inclusion or  combined probability of  exclusion um we have abandoned that  approach now  again and we're using these computer  software programs so we still have the  human dna analyst that is  extracting the dna from the evidence  doing all of the laboratory work to  generate the dna profiles but once  once the dna mixture profile is  developed and produced  then we're letting a computer software  program  analyze the data instead of us  doing it as human so i'll take you back  to  this previous slide in the presentation  where  recall that at the time of mr grant's  original trial in 2011  they had a dna mixture that was  recovered from the fingernails  of the murder victim and they were not  able to  draw any conclusions about whether mr  grant was included or executed from that  mixture  so they just called it inconclusive and  so  in february 2019 is when i started  consulting on this case and of course  i'm familiar with the nrc report the  pcash report etc  so i thought okay well maybe you know  this technology was not available to  interpret dna mixtures at the time of  the trial but let's  see what these probabilistic genotyping  software programs will say  now about whether mr grant  is included or exploited from that  mixture  so before i sent  the raw data that was originally  generated back in 2011  for probabilistic genotyping analysis i  thought let me just do  kind of a quick screen let me take that  dna mixture  that was produced from the evidence in  2011 and let me just  list the alleles in a basic excel  spreadsheet  and this is what i did so just kind of  at first glance what does it look like  could he be possibly in this mixture  could he not  and you'll see here so on the far left  hand of the screen  you'll see a column called locus that's  the name of the dna marker  that was tested you've got lydell grants  individual dna profile that we obtained  from his reference sample  we've got the in the third column the  victims dna profile  from their reference sample and then  [Music]  the subsequent six columns are the  alleles that were present in that dna  mixture  and what i wanted to see was obviously  is we would expect the victim's dna to  be present in there but  is are there some common alleles with  lionel grant  and what i started seeing was just on  this initial screening  um of course this is not the way we  analyze dna mixtures but this is kind of  a first glance  approach it was pretty obvious to me  that mr grant his dna is not present in  this mixture so everything that's  highlighted in yellow here for you  and in red color  are alleles that were present in the  mixture  that the dna mixture that was recovered  from the victims fingernails that do not  correspond to alleles in  the victim's dna profile or mr grant's  dna profile and there were 26  what we call foreign alleles that were  present underneath the murder victim's  fingernails that don't match the murder  victim  or mr grant or the suspect the guy that  was convicted of this crime  so i should point out here that this  is something that took me about 30  minutes to do  that could have very easily have been  put together  and shown during lydell grant's original  trial in 2011  that i'll point out that lydell grant  was an indigent  client an indigent defendant he had a  [Music]  public defender defending him and that  public defender did not hire  a forensic dna expert on mr grant's  behalf  so all the jury heard was  what's on this previous slide that there  was a dna mixture recovered  from the murder victim's fingernails and  the dna mixture's too complex we can't  interpret it so we're going to call it  inconclusive  but then of course they heard that there  were six eyewitnesses that identified mr  grant  now with that being said back to this  slide again this took me 30 minutes to  put together  i think even in 2011 before we were  using these  computer software programs to analyze  dna mixtures  had mr grant's attorney hired a dna  expert this  might have and they had posted this up  on the screen  so the jury could see it this might have  planted that reasonable doubt  in the minds of the jury that they had  the  you know whether or not they had the  correct person  this might might have been enough  to plant that reasonable doubt and  remember the burden of proof in a  criminal trial is you have to think that  someone is guilty beyond a reasonable  doubt  there's clearly someone else  present underneath the fingernails of  the murder victim just by looking at  this  basic screening tool so i was working  with  mike ware who's the director of the  innocence project of texas he's a  defense attorney so i call him  he's in texas i'm in connecticut and i  send him a screenshot of this and i'm  like well you know you're right  i don't think lydell is in the mixture  um but  what we need to do based on the current  recommendations of the pcas report  and all the national audits that are  happening is we can't just go with this  we need to now  send this raw data this dna mixture data  and have it analyzed with one of these  probabilistic genotyping software  program  so we sent the raw data to a company  called cyber genetics in pennsylvania  and this company is the developer the  maker  of true allele dna interpretation  software  and what you can do so this is march of  2019  you can submit the dna data  that that contains the data for the dna  mixture as  well as the dna profiles of the victim  and the suspect and they will run it  through their  probabilistic genotyping software and  they will send you back a report  and tell you based on that based on  their software analysis  who's included in the mixture and who's  excluded  and you get it in an email response and  basically you can see here this is  actually a screenshot  of the email that i got back in march  2019 saying  um if you look at the highlighted region  victim erin sherhorn's dna was found on  his fingernails well we knew that  because they are aaron sherhorn's  fingernails but the very next bullet  point there you'll notice  is defendant lydell grant's dna was  not found on the victim's fingernail so  he's  excluded as a contributor to that dna  mixture  and then they go on to state dna from an  unknown person  was found on the victim's fingernails  and they  again subtract the  victim's dna profile out of the mixture  because you know that person's dna  is in the mixture and they essentially  the soccer program essentially deduced  the alleles that are in the dna profile  of the foreign contributor  and um so we got excited at that point  we thought we knew we were moving in the  right direction and that they had indeed  convicted the wrong  person but on top of the email  that you saw in the previous slide that  states who's  included and who's excluded from a dna  mixture  one of the other things that cyber  genetics will do  is they apply a statistical weight  to that inclusion or exclusion and you  can see  item 12.2.1.1 is the victim's fingernail  we also had a dna profile from the  victim  a reference sample a victim or i'm sorry  dna profile  from lydell grant the accused and  they apply a statistical probability so  you can see  this is the exponent that 19 underneath  victim  that's the exponent for the statistical  value so you can see  um um this is somewhere between  obviously the victim's dna is included  somewhere up in the quintillion  septillion  numbers um he's obviously included we  expected that  but look at lydell grant's  reference dna profile he is excluded  um between that billions and trillions  there's 8.5 billion individuals on  planet earth  so this exceeds the popul the human  population of earth  and then the unknown male contributor  who's present in that dna mixture  both the unknown male contributor and  the victim are  determined to be the two individuals in  that mixture they're included in the  mixture  lido grant is excellent so  [Music]  we thought at that point that you know  that was our k  that was our grounds for appeal you know  we've proven that  lidell grant's dna is not underneath the  fingernails of the murder victim  and we contacted the conviction  integrity unit at the houston da's  office  we wanted to move forward with the  appeal  and then the thought process became well  so what  you know aaron shearhorn was  out in the bar district on a saturday  night in houston  um he's a gay man he might have had  a sexual encounter earlier that evening  and that  foreign contributor that unknown dna  profile underneath his fingernails  might not be related to the murder at  all it might be related to  a sexual encounter that he had earlier  that evening  so we knew we had to find out  [Music]  who this dna profile belonged to  so in april of 2019 we worked with  john donahue at beaufort county  sheriff's office in south carolina  to have a to have this unknown dna  profile  that was found in the mixture searched  against the fbi's codis dna database  and we  got results back a couple of months  later in june 2019 that we had a hit  in the codis database and let me remind  you  that the codis the national dna database  maintained by the fbi is vastly  incomplete  because it only contains the dna  profiles of convicted offenders so  people that have been caught  and it contains for some state it  allows us to profile the dna of all  arrestees so people that have been  arrested but not yet convicted  so anybody that hasn't been arrested  hasn't been caught and convicted will  not be in this database so there's a  chance that we could have not  had a hit at all or match in the  database at all  we did get a hit to an individual named  americo carter and  not only had he been in and out of the  prison system throughout his life he's  like habitual criminal  but he was we did some background  investigation on him  he was known to not be incarcerated at  the time mr sheerhorn was murdered  he lived in the houston area where he  where mr sherhorn was murdered during  that time period and he moved away  from houston a couple of months after  the crime occurred  he was actually tracked down in december  in atlanta georgia and arrested  for the murder of mr sherhorn and he has  actually confessed to the crime  so june 2019 is when we identified  jericho  carter as the perpetrator and  it took us several more months after  that to get  lydell grant out of prison so this is an  image  in the top image on the far left that's  mike ware the director of  the innocence project of texas and you  can tell lydell is happy  in this photo this was right when he  found out he  was going to get released from prison  this was the day before thanksgiving so  late november 2019 and then the bottom  photo  is him walking out of the courthouse  after he's been released from prison  and that's his mother and his brother  that he is with  and then so that again he was released  from prison the day before thanksgiving  and then december is when they finally  tracked down at this point we were going  to be preparing for a new trial  for lydell so we could present all the  new  dna evidence probabilistic genotyping  analysis and all the codex hit and  everything  and in the meantime the police were  searching for  jamerrica carter that we identified in  the  fbi's database and these are some  screenshots of some articles that came  out  he was arrested new suspect arrested  charged in 2010 montrose stabbing  wanted murder hid in atlanta for years  while an innocent man  sat behind bars and then i just wanted  to show you  an image here again on the left this is  lydell grant's  mech shot when he was arrested in 2010  and on the far right is jamerrica carter  when he was arrested in december 2019  and you know you can study you can go  back there are multiple instances  throughout history  of where dna has been used to exonerate  someone that was originally convicted  based on eyewitness identification  um and if you end up looking at an image  of the convicted person and the true  perpetrator that was later convicted  there do tend to be some similarities  in the way they look um  as far as this happening and with  wrongful convictions you can look at the  national registry of  since 1989 there have been  2 611 exonerations  and the combined imprisonment time more  than 23  000 years lost the people that were  wrongfully convicted  and i don't you know i'm not going to  spend a lot of time on all of these  steps but  this graph here talks about  um the various contributing factors to  wrongful convictions  and a particularly circled mistaken  witness id  which was a factor in lydell's wrongful  conviction  and false or misleading forensic  evidence again  there wasn't false evidence in this it  just was that there was a dna mixture  recovered  and it was called inconclusive and the  way it was presented in court was  misleading and kind of left the door  open  for the jury to interpret  that he possibly could be in the mixture  and then again compounded with the six  eyewitnesses that identified him that  kind of sealed his state with that  conviction  and so i just want to point out here  lastly that  um you know the the nrc the 2009 nrc  report and the 2016  pcash report were geared towards  they're not pro-defense reports  they are definitely pro-science  pro-justice report  and our goal in the justice system  whether we are  attorneys or forensic scientists should  be  to find the guilty and protect the  innocent we don't want the  cases like this to happen where someone  spends 10 years in prison  for a crime they didn't commit so  um oops sorry so  you know we've we've had this mindset we  need to go back to this there's a 19th  century medical legalist  that said you know if the law has made  you a 